1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Vittoria Colonna''' (April 1490 – 25 February 1547), marchioness of Pescara, was an Italian noblewoman and poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Vittoria Colonna
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady, how can it chance - yet this we see
2 CONT  In long experience - that will longer last
2 CONT  A living image carved from quarries vast
2 CONT  than its own maker, who dies presently?
2 CONT  Cause yieldeth to effect if this so be,
2 CONT  And even Nature is by Art surpassed;
2 CONT  This know I, who to Art have given the past,
2 CONT  But see that Time is breaking faith with me.
2 CONT  Perhaps on both of us long life can I
2 CONT  Either in color or in stone bestow,
2 CONT  By now portraying each in look and mien;
2 CONT  So that a thousand years after we die,
2 CONT  How fair thou wast, and I how full of woe,
2 CONT  And wherefore I so loved thee, may be seen.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Seven Sonnets and a Canzone by Michelangelo (di Lodovico Buonarroti Simoni)
2 CONT  Translated by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow
2 CONT
2 CONT More...[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vittoria_Colonna Wiki - Vittoria Colonna]
2 CONT
