2 CONT
2 CONT Niccolò III. d’Este (* 1383 oder 1384; † 26. Dezember 1441) war das einzige Kind des Markgrafen Alberto I. d’Este von Ferrara, Modena und Reggio, dem er 1393 nachfolgte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niccolò heiratete dreimal:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * im Juni 1397 Cecilia di Carrara († 1416), eine Tochter Francescos di Carrara,
2 CONT
2 CONT     * am 2. April 1418 Parasina Malatesta († 1425), eine Tochter Malatesta Malatestas,
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1431 Ricarda di Saluzzo († 1474), eine Tochter von Tommaso III, Markgraf von Saluzzo
2 CONT
2 CONT Aus diesen drei Ehen und aus einer Reihe außerehelicher Verbindungen hatte er mindestens 24 Kinder:
2 CONT
2 CONT #      Camilla d’Este ∞ 1448 Rodolfo von Camerino († 1464),
2 CONT #      Fulco d’Este († 1433),
2 CONT #       Alberto d’Este († 1450),
2 CONT #      Isotta d’Este (1403–1404),
2 CONT #      Ugo d’Este (1405–1425),
2 CONT #      Meliaduso d’Este (1406–1452), Abt in Pomposa und Ferrara, tritt 1425 zurück,
2 CONT #      Leonello d’Este (1407–1450) ∞ 1) 1435 Margherita Gonzaga († 1439), Tochter Francesco Gonzagas, ∞ 2) 1444 Maria († 1449), uneheliche Tochter König Alfons’ V. von Aragón,
2 CONT #       Margherita d’Este ∞ 1427 Galeotto Roberto Malatesta,
2 CONT #       Margherita d’Este († 1452) ∞ Galasso Pio,
2 CONT #       Margherita d’Este ∞ Franceschino zu Verona,
2 CONT #       Borso d’Este (1413–1471), Herzog von Modena und Reggio 1452, Herzog von Ferrara 1471,
2 CONT #        Viridis d’Este, Nonne,
2 CONT #       Alberto d’Este (1415–1502),
2 CONT #       Gurone d’Este († 1484),
2 CONT #       Lucia d’Este (1419–1437) ∞ 1437 Carlo Gonzaga († 1456), Sohn Gianfrancesco Gonzagas,
2 CONT #       Ginevra d’Este (1419–1440) ∞ 1433 Sigismondo Malatesta (1417–1468),
2 CONT #       Alberto Carlo d’Este (*/† 1421),
2 CONT #       Isotta d’Este (1425–1456) ∞ 1) 1444 Oddo Antonio da Montefeltro (1426–1444), ∞ 2) 1446 Stefano Frangipani,
2 CONT #        Beatrice d’Este (1427–1497) ∞ 1) 1448 Niccolò von Correggio († 1449), ∞ 2) 1451 Tristano Sforza († 1477)
2 CONT #       Ercole I. d’Este (1431–1505), Herzog 1471, ∞ 1473 Eleonora von Neapel (1450–1493), Tochter König Ferdinands I.,
2 CONT #       Sigismondo d’Este (1433–1507), Markgraf von San Martino seit 1501,
2 CONT #        Rinaldo d’Este (um 1435–1503) ∞ 1472 Lucrezia von Montferrat († nach 1481), Tochter des Markgrafen Wilhelm X.,
2 CONT #       Bianca d’Este (1440–1506) ∞ 1468 Galeotto Pico, Herr von Mirandola († 1499),
2 CONT #        Orsina d’Este ∞ 1) Aldobrandino Ragnoni († nach 1454), ∞ 2) NN Malatesta, ∞ 3) 1469 Andrea Gualengo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ein Vierteljahr nach seiner Thronbesteigung im Frühjahr 1433 bestätigte der römisch-deutsche Kaiser Sigismund am 17. September 1433 dem Markgrafen Niccolò III. d'Este die Grafschaft Comacchio als zusätzliches Reichslehen, nachdem 
2 CONC er bereits am 13. September dessen Söhne zu Rittern geschlagen hatte.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Vorgänger
2 CONT
2 CONT Alberto I. d’Este
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Herr von Modena
2 CONT
2 CONT 1393–1441 	Nachfolger
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonello d’Este
2 CONT
2 CONT Einzelnachweise [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ↑ Joseph Aschbach: Geschichte Kaiser Sigmunds, 4. Band: Sigmunds letzte Regierungsjahre zur Zeit des Baseler Konzils, Hamburg 1845, S. 469 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Diese Seite wurde zuletzt am 30. März 2010 um 10:03 Uhr geändert
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Estei Miklos őrgróf az Esta fejedelmi család tagja.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naturális lánya Estei Isotta.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Born in Ferrara, the son of Alberto d'Este and Isotta Albaresani, he inherited the rule of the city when he was still 10 years old, under the protection of the Republics of Venice, Florence and Bologna. He was attacked by his rela
2 CONC tive Azzo X d'Este, a general of Gian Galeazzo Visconti, Duke of Milan, a descendant of Obizzo II d'Este who contested Niccolò the right to rule in Ferrara due to his illegitimate birth[1]. Azzo was however taken prisoner by Astor
2 CONC re I Manfredi in the ensuing war, and any menace over Niccolò's rule disappeared. In 1397 he married Gigliola da Carrara, daughter of Francesco II da Carrara, lord of Padua. In 1403 he joined the league formed against the Duke of 
2 CONC Milan, being appointed commander-in-chief of the Papal Army by Pope Boniface IX. In 1405 he ceded the ancestral family lands near Este to Venice. In 1410 he commissioned three copies of Fior di Battaglia. This manuscript is a larg
2 CONC e part of the foundation of modern attempts to rebuild the Western martial arts. In 1413 he made a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. In 1418 he remarried to Parisina Malatesta, daughter of Andrea Malatesta. Fearing the ambitions of Fil
2 CONC ippo Maria Visconti, two years later he ceded him the possession of Parma. In 1425 Niccolò had both his wife Parisina and his illegitimate son Ugo executed on charges of adultery. In that year he was again commander-in-chief of th
2 CONC e anti-Visconti league. In 1429 his illegitimate son was named heir of the Marquisate. The role of Niccolò as a prestigious leader in Italy was confirmed when his city was chosen as the seat of a council in 1438. [edit] Marriages 
2 CONC and children He married first Gigliola da Carrara, daughter of Francesco II da Carrara, lord of Padua in June, 1397. She died of the plague in 1416. They had no known children. He married secondly Parisina Malatesta, daughter of A
2 CONC ndrea Malatesta. He had her executed on 21 May 1425 for allegedly having an affair with his illegitimate son Ugo. They had three children: Ginevra d'Este (born 1419). Married her maternal relative Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta. Lu
2 CONC zia d'Este (24 March 1419 - 28 June 1437). Married Carlo Gonzaga of Milan, Lord of Sabbioneta. Alberto Carlo d'Este (born and deceased in 1421). He married thirdly Ricciardia of Saluzzo in 1429. She was a daughter of Thomas III of
2 CONC  Saluzzo and Marguerite of Roussy. They had two children: Ercole d'Este I ((October 26, 1431 – June 15, 1505). Sigimondo d'Este (1433 - 1 April 1507). He also had eleven illegitimate children: Ugo d'Este. Executed by his father on
2 CONC  21 May 1425 for allegedly having an affair with his stepmother Parisina Malatesta Meliaduse d'Este, Abbot of Pomposa and Ferrara, (1406 - 1452). Son by Caterina of Medici. Leonello d'Este (1407 - 1450). Son by Stella de' Tolomei.
2 CONC  Borso d'Este (1413 - 1471). Son by Stella de' Tolomei. Alberto d'Este (1415 - 8 April 1502). Son by Filippa della Tavola. Isotta d'Este (1425 - 1456). Daughter by Filippa della Tavola. Married first Oddantonio da Montefeltro , Du
2 CONC ke of Urbino and secondly Stephen Frangipani, Count of Segni. Beatrice d'Este (1427 - 1497). Married Niccolò of Correggio. Rinaldo d'Este, Lord of Ostellato (c. 1435 - 1535). Son by Anna de Roberti. Branca Maria d'Este (18 Decembe
2 CONC r 1440 - 12 January 1506). Daughter by Anna de Roberti. Married Galeotto I Pico, Lord della Mirandola. Gurone d'Este (d. 1484). An Abbot. Camilla d'Este. Married Rodolfo da Varano of Camerino.
2 CONT
