1 NAME Claude /de Rye de la Palud/
2 GIVN Claude
2 SURN de Rye de la Palud
2 _MARNM de Rye de la Palud
1 NAME Claude de Rye de la Palud, Baron of Valançon
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1576
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1648
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valan%C3%A7on
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude de Rye de la Palud, Baron of Valançon (Bouligneux, Bresse, c. 1576 — Namur, 24-III-1648) was a General of the Artillery in Flanders under the service of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Philibert de Rye (1540–1597), count of Varax and of de La Roche-St. Hippolyte, baron of Balançon and of Romange, lord of Vuillefans, and of Claudine de Tournon-Roussillon, mistress of Vassoulieu.
2 CONT
2 CONT Serving in a Burgundian regiment under the orders of his brother Christophe, he took part in the siege of Ostende (1601–1604) where he lost a leg in the 26th July 1601. Thereafter he was provided with a wooden leg.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 24th July 1602 his regiment was converted into a Tercio and he succeeded his brother as "maestre de campo" thus becoming, although with interruptions, the commander of that unit for the next 23 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1620 he participated in the invasion of the Palatinate and took part of the defence of Alzey (14-XI-1620), where his Burgundian Tercio managed to repel every enemy attack until being relieved by the count Hendrik van den Berg, 
2 CONC Cavalry General in the Army of the Palatinate. He was also present at the Battle of Fleurus (29.VIII 1622), the siege of Berg-Op-Zoom and the siege of Breda (1624–25), city of which he became Governor until 1631.
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year he was nominated Captain-General and Great Master of the Artillery in the Army of Flanders, substituting Hendrik van den Berg, now General of the Cavalry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1635 he ravaged the French regions of Artoys and Picardie, using Croatian troops.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1636, with 2,000 horses and 10,000 infantry soldiers, he reppeled a Dutch force sent to storm Juliers and Cleves, thereafter relieving Schenck, besieged by the Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude kept his position in the Army until 1638, when he became a member of the War Council in Brussels. In 1645 he was designated to the post of Captain-General of Namur, living the last three years of his life in the castle of N
2 CONC amur.
