1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessandro_Farnese_(cardinal)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alessandro Farnese (5 October 1520 – 2 March 1589), an Italian cardinal and diplomat and a great collector and patron of the arts, was the grandson of Pope Paul III (who also bore the name Alessandro Farnese), and the son of Pier 
2 CONC Luigi Farnese, Duke of Parma, who was murdered in 1547.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at Valentano (current province of Viterbo), he studied at Bologna, and was appointed administrator of the Diocese of Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 December 1534, at the age of 14, he was appointed Cardinal Deacon of the Title of Sant'Angelo by Paul III, his grandfather, who had been elected to the papacy two months previously.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gran Cardinale received many other offices and benefices, becoming Vice-Chancellor of the Holy Roman Church, Governor of Tivoli, Archpriest of St. Mary Major Basilica, Archpriest of St. Peter's Basilica, Administrator of Jaen,
2 CONC  Spain, of Vizeu, Portugal, of Würzburg[citation needed], Germany and of Avignon, France. In 1536 he became Bishop of Monreale, Sicily; after he inherited estate after the murder of his father (1547), in 1552 he founded a Jesuit c
2 CONC ollege there.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became Bishop of Massa in 1538, Archbishop of Tours in 1553, and Bishop of Cahors; Archbishop of Benevento, and Cardinal-Bishop of Ostia and Velletri and Dean of the College of Cardinals in 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also became a Papal Legate, arranging peace between the perpetually warring Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor and Francis I of France. In 1546 he accompanied the troops sent by the pope to the aid of Charles V against the Schmalkal
2 CONC dic League. In 1580, he was an unsuccessful candidate for the papacy. Among the buildings that Cardinal Farnese built or restored are the Church of the Gesù in Rome, the Villa Farnese at Caprarola, and the Farnese palace near Lake
2 CONC  Bracciano, and the monastery Tre Fontane.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alessandro Farnese is remembered for gathering the greatest collection of Roman sculpture assembled in private hands since Antiquity, now mostly in Naples, after passing by inheritance to the Bourbon-Parma kings. His generosity to
2 CONC wards artists made a virtual academy at the power house he built at Caprarola and in his lodgings at Palazzo della Cancellaria and, after his brother Cardinal Ranuccio Farnese died in 1565, at the Palazzo Farnese. In the Palazzo F
2 CONC arnese the best sculptors worked under his eye, to restore fragments of antiquities as complete sculptures, with great scholarly care. He was also a great patron of living artists. Under the direction of his curator and librarian,
2 CONC  the antiquarian iconographer Fulvio Orsini, the Farnese collections were enlarged and systematised. Farnese collected ancient coins and commissioned modern medals. He had paintings by Titian, Michelangelo, and Raphael, and an imp
2 CONC ortant collection of drawings. He commissioned the masterpiece of Giulio Clovio, arguably the last major illuminated manuscript, the Farnese Hours, which was completed in 1546 after being nine years in the making (now Morgan Libra
2 CONC ry, New York). The studiolo built to house this collection appears to be the one re-erected at the Musée de la Renaissance, Écouen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1550, Farnese acquired a northern portion of Palatine hill in Rome and had Roman ruins from the time of Tiberius at the northwest end filled in and* converted to a summer home and formal gardens. The Farnese Gardens became one 
2 CONC of the first botanical gardens in Europe. From these gardens are derived the names of Acacia farnesiana and from its floral essence, the important biochemical farnesol.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cardinal's only daughter, Clelia, married firstly Giangiorgio Cesarini, Marchese of Civitanova, and secondly Marco Pio di Savoia, Lord of Sassuolo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farnese was buried before the high altar in the Church of Gesù.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Farnese-20
2 CONT * https://archive.org/details/miscmss_farnese_a
2 CONT * https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/05788b.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/38699897/alessandro-farnese
2 CONT
2 CONT Clergy. He was the eldest son of Pier Luigi Farnese, Duke of Parma and Girolama Orsini. His paternal grandfather Paul III, who had recently become pope, created him a cardinal at the age of fourteen in December 1534. After a year,
2 CONC  he was named vice-chancellor of the church, a position he held until his death. In 1539, he was named papal legate to re-establish the peace between the emperor and Francois I of France. It was through his mediation that the two 
2 CONC sovereigns met in Paris in 1544. A patron of literature, art, and science, he commissioned Vignola to construct Il Gesù, the Palazzo Farnese in Rome, and the Villa Farnese in Caprarola.
