1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ferrante II Gonzaga (1563 – 6 August 1630) was count and, from 1621, duke of Guastalla.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferrante II Gonzaga
2 CONT He was the son of Cesare I Gonzaga, count of Guastalla and duke of Amalfi, and Donna Camilla Borromeo.[1] He succeeded his father in 1575. On 2 July 1621, the County of Guastalla was elevated to a duchy and Ferrante was subsequent
2 CONC ly deemed a duke.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1624 Emperor Ferdinand II appointed Ferrante as general commissar in Italy to reinforce imperial authority.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferrante played a part in the War of the Mantuan Succession when, as a distant Gonzaga cousin, he claimed the Duchy of Mantua after the extinction of the senior male branch of the House of Gonzaga in December 1627. He was nominall
2 CONC y supported by Emperor Ferdinand II,[4] who really sought to re-attach the Duchy of Mantua to the Holy Roman Empire. His attempt failed as the French candidate Charles of Nevers became the new duke. Ferrante died of the plague on 
2 CONC 6 August 1630.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage & issue
2 CONT Ferrante II married Vittoria Doria (1569–1618), daughter of Giovanni Andrea Doria,[6] and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cesare II Gonzaga (1592–1632), next Duke of Guastalla, married Donna Isabella Orsini[6]
2 CONT Vincenzo Gonzaga (1602–1697), Viceroy of Sicily (1677–1678)[6]
2 CONT Andrea Gonzaga, Count of San Paolo (died 1686), father of Vincenzo Gonzaga, Duke of Guastalla (1692–1714)[6]
2 CONT Zenobia de Gonzaga y Doria (1588–1618), married in 1607 don Giovanni Tagliavia d'Aragona, Duke of Terranova.[6]
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Parrott 1997, p. 152-153.
2 CONT  Grendler 2009, p. 3.
2 CONT  Runschke 2019, p. 214.
