1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Portrait of Eleonora d` Aragona from " Il modo di regere e di regnare " by Antonio Cornazzano.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonora of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonora of Naples or Eleonora of Aragon (June 22, 1450 – October 11, 1493) was the first Duchess of Ferrara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Ferdinand I of Naples and Isabella of Taranto , she was firstly consort of Massimiliano Sforza, Duke of Bari.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 3, 1473, she married Ercole I d'Este, Duke of Ferrara.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her journey to Ferrara she was accompanied by a group made up of some of the most important names in the Estense court.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding ceremony with Ercole I was followed by a long series of celebrations in her honour, with dances, tournaments, processions and lunches.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ercole and Eleonora had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella (1474-1539), who would later prove to be one of the most powerful women of the Renaissance
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice (1475-1497)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso (1476-1534), who would later marry the infamous Lucrezia Borgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferrante (1477-1540),
2 CONT
2 CONT Ippolito (1479-1520), cardinal, army commander and patron of the arts
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismondo (1480-1524)
2 CONT
2 CONT She held firmly on to the reins of government during her husband's absences, showing herself to be decisive and authoritative, but also wise and level-headed.
