1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrea_Acciaioli
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Andrea Acciaioli''' was an Italian noblewoman, as the Countess of Altavilla in the 14th century. Born in Florence she was a friend of [https://www.geni.com/people/Giovanni-Boccaccio/6000000012438622868 Giovanni Boccaccio], and 
2 CONC the person to whom he dedicated his book [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Mulieribus_Claris ''On Famous Women''].[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT   Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrea was the sister of Niccolò Acciaioli, the daughter of Acciaiolo, a wealthy Florentine merchant connected to the Acciaioli family of Florence. Her family was an important family of Italy from the twelfth century and associate
2 CONC d with banking. She was a member of the court of Joanna, Queen of Naples.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first husband was Carlo d'Arto, Count of Monteodorisio, who died in 1346. Sometime after 1358 and before 1362 she then married Bartolomeo II of Capua, Count of Altavilla.[3] She was described as being of strong moral character
2 CONC , highly educated, and having elegant speech.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Giovanni-Boccaccio/6000000012438622868 Giovanni Boccaccio] le ha dedicato la sua opera letteraria [https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_mulieribus_claris ''De mulieribus claris''], vedi: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_mulieribus_claris
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://www.genmarenostrum.com/pagine-lettere/letteraa/ACCIAIOLI2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT C3. Andreina o Andriana (vediiv. 1373)
2 CONT
2 CONT #  = a) Carlo d’Artus, Conte di Monteodorisio (+decapitato nel 1345) (vedi/see)
2 CONT
2 CONT #   = b) 13-X-1353 Bartolomeo II di Capua, 2° Conte d’Altavilla (*1325 ca., +post 1394) (vedi/see)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
