1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludovico_II_of_Saluzzo
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludovico II del Vasto (March 23, 1438, Saluzzo – January 27, 1504) was marquess of Saluzzo from 1475 until his death. Before his accession ar marquis he held the title of Count of Carmagnola.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Ludovico I of Saluzzo and Isabella of Monferrat, he succeeded him as marquis upon the latter's death in 1475. He continued his father's war against Charles I of Savoy, which had depleted Saluzzo's fortunes, but again withou
2 CONC t notable results.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1481 he married his cousin, Giovanna of Monferrat. Two years after her death in 1490, he remarried, to Margaret of Foix-Candale.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Second Italian War, Ludovico supported Charles VIII of France in his invasion of Italy, fighting alongside him in the battle of Fornovo (1495). When Louis XII of France invaded the Duchy of Milan, Ludovico was again on 
2 CONC the French side, culminating in his defeat at the Battle of Garigliano in 1503 against the Spanish forces. His military expenses further denied any possibility of recovery to the exhausted marquisate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludovico was succeeded by his and Marguerite's eldest child, Michele Antonio I of Saluzzo, in 1504. A monument to him is located in the church of San Giovanni at Saluzzo, commissioned by his wife Margaret and executed by Benedetto
2 CONC  Briosco in 1508. Ludovico II was also the initiator of the Neo-Gothic Cathedral of Saluzzo and of the first Alpine tunnel, the Buco di Viso (2,880 meters over the sea level).
