2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco I of Tuscany as a young boy; painting by Bronzino
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Florence, he was the son of Cosimo I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany and Eleonora di Toledo, and served as regent for his father starting in 1564.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage to Joanna of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 18, 1565, he married Joanna of Austria, youngest daughter of Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor and Anna of Bohemia and Hungary, after among others Princess Elizabeth of Sweden had been considered. By all reports, it was 
2 CONC not a happy marriage. Joanna was homesick for her native Austria, and Francesco was neither charming nor faithful. Joanna died at the age of thirty one in 1578.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Bianca Cappello
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after the Grand Duchess Joanna had died, Francesco went on to marry his Venetian mistress, Bianca Cappello, after aptly disposing of her husband, a Florentine bureaucrat. Because of the quick remarriage and similar occurrence
2 CONC s among the Medici (Francesco's younger brother Pietro had reportedly killed his wife), rumors spread up that Francesco and Bianca had conspired to poison Johanna. Francesco reportedly built and decorated Villa Medicea di Pratolin
2 CONC o for Bianca. She was, however, not always popular among Florentines. They had no children, but Francesco adopted her daughter by first marriage Pellegrina (1564- ?) and her son Antonio (August 29, 1576 - May 2, 1621), who was fir
2 CONC st adopted as newborn child by Bianca Cappello with the intention to present him to Francesco as "own child" by means of changeling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, Francesco was often despotic, but while Cosimo had known how to maintain Florentine independence, Francesco acted more like a vassal of his father-in-law, the emperor, and subsequent Holy Roman Emperors. He contin
2 CONC ued the heavy taxation of his subjects in order to pay large sums to the empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had an amateur's interest in manufacturing and sciences. He founded porcelain and stoneware manufacture, but these did not thrive until after his death. He continued his father's patronage of the arts, supporting artists and bu
2 CONC ilding the Medici Theater as well as founding the Accademia della Crusca. He was also passionately interested in chemistry and alchemy and spent many hours in his private laboratory/curio collection, the Studiolo in the Palazzo Ve
2 CONC cchio, which held his collections of natural item and stones and allowed him to dabble in amateur chemistry and alchemical schemes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco and Bianca died on the same day, possibly poisoned, or as many historians believe, from malarial fever. However, recent forensic evidence uncovered by Italian scientists supports the theory that he and his wife were pois
2 CONC oned.[1] Francesco was succeeded by his younger brother, Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a famous portrait of Francesco as a child by Agnolo Bronzino, which hangs in the Uffizi Gallery in Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco's marriage to Bianca and the couple's death was exploited by Thomas Middleton for his tragedy Women Beware Women (published 1657).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Francesco and Johanna had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eleonora (March 1, 1566 – September 9, 1611), who married Vincenzo I Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua (1582-1612).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Romola (November 20, 1568 – December 2, 1568)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anna (December 31, 1569 – February 19, 1584)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isabella (September 30, 1571 – August 8, 1572)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Lucrezia (November 7, 1572 – August 14, 1574)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marie (1573 – 1642), who became Queen of France by her marriage to Henri IV in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Filippo (May 20, 1577 – March 29, 1582)
